-### Parameters
-- **Dimensions**: The binvox format requires cubic dimensions ($W=H=D$). The source examples use $512 \times 512 \times 512$ [S2, unofficial].
-- **RLE Pairs**: Each pair consists of a value (1 byte) and a count (1 byte) [S2, unofficial].
+## Subtopics
 
-### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-- **Whitespace Skipping**: When reading the binary RLE section, do not use text-based line reading or whitespace-skipping parsers. Zero bytes are valid RLE counts/values and must be read exactly [S1, unofficial].
-- **Dimension Support**: The source implementation hardcodes `512` and panics on other dimensions [S2, unofficial]. General implementations should parse the dimension from the header and validate it is cubic [S1, unofficial].
-- **Capacity Overflow**: If the RLE data specifies a total count greater than $D^3$, the parser must abort [S2, unofficial].
+### [SVO Construction](link-to-svo-construction.md)
+Builds a Sparse Voxel Octree from the dense voxel data. Sets itself apart by using a hierarchical structure for memory efficiency. Choose when memory is constrained or for ray tracing.
+
+### [Schematic Conversion](link-to-schematic-conversion.md)
+Converts binvox data into a Schematic object for game engines. Sets itself apart by mapping voxels to color palettes and world coordinates. Choose when exporting to Minecraft-like engines.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity**: $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of RLE pairs [G]. Decompression is linear in the size of the RLE data [G]. SVO construction is $O(D^3)$ in the worst case (fully occupied) but proportional to the number of occupied voxels and tree depth [G].
-- **Memory Complexity**: $O(D^3)$ for the flat array [G]. The SVO structure adds overhead for node objects, potentially reducing memory usage for sparse scenes compared to a dense grid, but increasing it for dense scenes due to node metadata [G].
-- **Performance**: The RLE format is significantly more compact than raw voxels for sparse data. Decompression is fast. Building the SVO adds overhead but enables efficient ray traversal [G].
-- **Strengths**: Compact storage for sparse voxel data; simple binary format.
-- **Limitations**: Fixed dimension support in some implementations; RLE can be inefficient for highly complex, non-repetitive data; no color/normal data in the basic format (though extensions exist) [S1, S2, unofficial].
+- **Time Complexity:** $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of voxels ($dim^3$). RLE decompression is linear in the size of the compressed data, which is $\le N$. SVO construction is $O(N)$ as each voxel is visited once.
+- **Memory Complexity:** $O(N)$ for the dense grid. $O(V)$ for SVO where $V$ is the number of occupied voxels (plus overhead for nodes).
+- **Measured Performance:** S2 uses 512x512x512 grids. S1 notes power-of-2 requirements as a TODO, implying potential optimization for such sizes.
+- **Strengths:** Compact storage via RLE; simple ASCII header for easy debugging.
+- **Limitations:** RLE is inefficient for sparse data with many single-voxel changes; fixed header structure limits metadata extensibility.
+- **When to Use:** Importing pre-computed voxel models from tools like MagicaVoxel.
+- **When Not to Use:** Real-time generation of voxel data; highly sparse large worlds (use SVO directly).
 
 ## Background
-The binvox format was developed for efficient storage and transmission of 3D voxel models, particularly in computer graphics and gaming. It uses a simple ASCII header followed by binary RLE data. The specific parsing method described here is from the `svo-cpu` project, which imports binvox files into a CPU-based Sparse Voxel Octree rasterizer [S2, unofficial].
+The binvox format was developed for efficient storage of voxel models, particularly for computer graphics and game development. It is widely used in conjunction with Sparse Voxel Octree (SVO) renderers [S1, S2, unofficial]. The format's simplicity (ASCII header, binary RLE payload) makes it a common interchange format. S2 and S3 demonstrate its use in CPU-based SVO rasterizers and schematic converters, respectively.
 
